from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which modern languages descend directly from Latin?","options":["Spanish, Italian, and French","German and Dutch","Russian and Polish","Arabic and Hebrew"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Latin''s everyday form evolved into the Romance languages, among them Spanish, Italian, and French - see ''Building sentences and the legacy of Latin''."},{"prompt":"Latin nouns change their endings to show their role in a sentence. This system of endings is called the system of...","options":["cases","tenses","genders","moods"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A noun''s case ending marks its job (subject, object, and so on) - see ''Nouns, gender, number, and cases''."},{"prompt":"The Latin verb form ''amo'' means...","options":["I love","to love","he loves","they love"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ending -o marks the first person singular, so amo = I love."},{"prompt":"In the sentence ''Servus puellam amat,'' the ending -am on ''puellam'' marks that word as the...","options":["subject","direct object","possessor","indirect object"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The accusative ending -am marks the direct object - the thing acted upon."},{"prompt":"The Latin verb ''sum'' means...","options":["I am","I have","I go","I give"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sum is the irregular verb ''to be'': sum = I am."},{"prompt":"In classical pronunciation, the letter ''v'' (as in ''veni'') was pronounced like...","options":["English w","English v","English f","it was silent"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Classical Latin v sounds like English w, so veni is ''WAY-nee'' - see ''The alphabet and classical pronunciation''."},{"prompt":"Latin word order can move around freely mainly because...","options":["the endings show each word''s job","Latin has no verbs","it is always subject-verb-object","the words never change form"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because case endings carry the grammar, the order of the words can vary without changing the meaning."},{"prompt":"The Latin word ''aqua'' (water) survives in the English word...","options":["aquarium","fire","mountain","bread"],"answer":0,"explanation":"English borrowed many Latin roots; aqua gives aquarium, aquatic, and aqueduct."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The alphabet and classical pronunciation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to read any Latin word aloud using classical (not church) pronunciation, name the vowels, and recognize the handful of consonants that differ from English. You will also know the simple rule that decides which syllable is stressed. Warm-up: how do you think the ''v'' in ''veni'' sounded to an ancient Roman - like our ''v'', or like something else? Latin spelling is remarkably regular. Once you learn the sound of each letter, you can pronounce a word you have never seen before, because the letters do not play tricks the way English letters often do. That reliability is one of the quiet pleasures of starting Latin, and it pays off immediately when you begin reading real sentences later in the course."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The classical Latin alphabet has 23 letters. There is no separate J or W, and U and V were originally the same letter. Every letter is pronounced; nothing is silent. The five vowels a, e, i, o, u each have a long and a short form, and vowel length can change a word''s meaning. Most consonants match English, with key exceptions: c is always hard, like k (never soft as in ''city''); g is always hard, as in ''get''; v is pronounced like English w; the letter i before a vowel acts as a consonant y, so ''iam'' sounds like ''yam''; r is lightly rolled; s is always hissed as in ''sit'' (never a z sound); and t is always a plain t (never ''sh''). Three common diphthongs: ae sounds like the ''y'' in ''my'', oe like ''oy'' in ''boy'', and au like ''ow'' in ''cow''. Stress normally falls on the second-to-last syllable."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Caesar in classical Latin is ''KYE-sar'': the c is hard like k and ae sounds like English ''eye'' - not the ''SEE-zer'' of later tradition. The famous line ''veni, vidi, vici'' is ''WAY-nee, WEE-dee, WEE-kee'', because v is w and c is k; it means ''I came, I saw, I conquered''. The word ''cibus'' (food) is ''KEE-bus'', again with a hard c. ''Magnus'' (great) is ''MAHG-nus'' with a hard g. The name ''puella'' (girl) has three syllables, poo-EL-la, with the stress on the second-to-last. ''Amamus'' (we love) is a-MA-mus, stressed on the long second-to-last syllable. ''Rosae'' (roses) is ''RO-sigh'', because the ae diphthong sounds like ''eye''. Say each of these aloud a few times until the hard consonants feel natural rather than forced."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check the answers. 1) How is the ''c'' pronounced in ''cena'' (dinner)? 2) What does the diphthong ''ae'' in ''puellae'' sound like? 3) Is any letter silent in the word ''est''? 4) How many syllables are in ''a-gri-co-la'' (farmer)? 5) How is the ''v'' in ''video'' (I see) pronounced? 6) How does ''iam'' (now, already) begin? Answers: 1) a hard k, so ''KAY-na''. 2) like English ''eye''. 3) no - classical Latin pronounces every letter, so you say ''est'' in full. 4) four syllables. 5) like English w, so ''WEE-deh-o''. 6) with a consonant y sound, so it sounds like ''yahm''. Notice how predictable each answer is once you trust the letters."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: softening c and g before e and i, giving church-Latin ''CHEH-na'' or a soft g - in classical Latin these letters stay hard, always k and hard g. Mistake: pronouncing v like the English v; in classical Latin it is the w sound. Mistake: treating vowel length as mere decoration - it can distinguish words, as with short ''os'' (bone) versus long ''os'' (mouth). Mistake: guessing the stress; place it on the second-to-last syllable in most ordinary words. Exit ticket: (a) What sound does classical ''v'' make? (b) Is ''c'' ever soft in classical Latin? (c) What does the diphthong ''ae'' sound like? Answers: the English ''w'' sound; no, it is always hard like k; it sounds like the English word ''eye''."}]}'::jsonb, 'In classical Latin every letter is pronounced; nothing is silent, and c and g are always hard.', 11),
  (3, 'Nouns: gender, number, and cases', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to explain what gender, number, and case mean in Latin, decline a first-declension noun in the nominative and accusative, and tell the subject from the object in a simple sentence no matter what order the words appear in. Warm-up: in English, how do we know that ''dog bites man'' differs from ''man bites dog''? We rely entirely on word order. Latin does something different and, once you see it, rather elegant: it tags each word with an ending that announces its role, which frees the word order to shift for emphasis or style without ever confusing who did what to whom."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every Latin noun carries three pieces of information. Gender is masculine, feminine, or neuter. Number is singular or plural. Case is an ending that marks the noun''s job in the sentence. English leans on word order; Latin leans on these endings. The two cases to learn first are the nominative, which marks the subject (the doer), and the accusative, which marks the direct object (the thing acted upon). First-declension nouns are mostly feminine and end in -a in the nominative singular. Their core endings are: nominative singular -a, accusative singular -am, nominative plural -ae, accusative plural -as. So puella means a girl as subject, puellam means a girl as object, puellae means girls as subject, and puellas means girls as object. Learn those four endings and a whole layer of Latin opens up to you."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Here is puella (girl) laid out as a small table in words: nominative singular puella, accusative singular puellam, nominative plural puellae, accusative plural puellas. Now a sentence: ''Puella rosam amat'' means ''The girl loves the rose'' - puella is the nominative subject, rosam the accusative object, amat the verb ''loves''. Move the words but keep the endings: ''Rosam puella amat'' means exactly the same thing, because the endings, not the order, carry the sense. Change the endings, however, and the meaning flips: ''Puellam rosa amat'' means ''The rose loves the girl''. In the plural, ''Puellae rosas portant'' means ''The girls carry the roses'' - puellae nominative plural, rosas accusative plural, portant ''they carry''. Read endings first, and the sentence falls into place."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Translate or answer, then check yourself. 1) Nautam puella spectat. 2) Give the accusative singular of ''terra'' (land). 3) Say ''The queen carries a crown'' using regina, corona, and portat. 4) Does ''Rosam puella amat'' mean the girl loves the rose, or the rose loves the girl? 5) What is the nominative plural of ''rosa''? Answers: 1) ''The girl watches the sailor'' - puella is nominative subject, nautam is accusative object. 2) terram. 3) Regina coronam portat. 4) the girl loves the rose, because puella is nominative and rosam is accusative. 5) rosae. If you read the endings before the word order, every one of these becomes straightforward."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading by word order and assuming the first noun must be the subject - always check the ending, not the position. Mistake: forgetting that -ae can be a nominative plural (and, as you will see later, a genitive or dative singular too), so let the verb and context guide you. Mistake: mixing up -am (accusative singular) with -as (accusative plural). Exit ticket: (a) Which case marks the subject of a sentence? (b) What is the accusative singular of ''puella''? (c) In ''Rosam puella amat'', which word is the subject? Answers: the nominative; puellam; puella, because it carries the nominative ending even though rosam comes first."}]}'::jsonb, 'Latin shows a word''s job with its ending, not its position; change the ending and you change the grammar, even if the word order stays the same.', 13),
  (4, 'Core vocabulary I: people and things', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"puella","translation":"girl","example":"Puella in silva ambulat. (The girl walks in the forest.)"},{"term":"servus","translation":"slave, servant","example":"Servus dominum laudat. (The servant praises the master.)"},{"term":"dominus","translation":"master, lord","example":"Dominus servos vocat. (The master calls the servants.)"},{"term":"amicus","translation":"friend","example":"Amicus poetam spectat. (The friend watches the poet.)"},{"term":"aqua","translation":"water","example":"Nauta aquam amat. (The sailor loves the water.)"},{"term":"terra","translation":"land, earth","example":"Agricola terram arat. (The farmer plows the land.)"},{"term":"rosa","translation":"rose","example":"Puella rosam portat. (The girl carries a rose.)"},{"term":"poeta","translation":"poet (masculine, though first declension)","example":"Poeta fabulam narrat. (The poet tells a story.)"},{"term":"regina","translation":"queen","example":"Regina magna est. (The queen is great.)"},{"term":"bellum","translation":"war (second-declension neuter)","example":"Bellum malum est. (War is bad.)"}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (5, 'Second-declension nouns', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to decline second-declension masculine nouns (ending in -us) and neuter nouns (ending in -um) in the nominative and accusative, both singular and plural, and combine them with the first-declension nouns you already know inside real sentences. Warm-up: if ''servus'' is the subject of a sentence, what ending would you expect when it becomes the object instead? You have already met this pattern with puella and puellam; the second declension works on the same principle of swapping endings, but with its own distinctive vowels. Learning it roughly doubles the stock of nouns you can read, because so many common Latin words are second declension."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The second declension holds most masculine nouns ending in -us and neuter nouns ending in -um. A masculine noun such as servus (servant) runs: nominative singular servus, accusative singular servum, nominative plural servi, accusative plural servos. A neuter noun such as bellum (war) runs: nominative singular bellum, accusative singular bellum (identical to the nominative), nominative plural bella, accusative plural bella (again identical). The neuter rule is worth memorizing right now: for any neuter noun the nominative and accusative are the same form, and both plurals end in -a. A few second-declension masculines end in -er rather than -us, such as puer (boy) and vir (man), but they still take -um in the accusative, giving puerum and virum."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"As tables in words: servus (servant) is nominative singular servus, accusative singular servum, nominative plural servi, accusative plural servos. Dominus (master) is dominus, dominum, domini, dominos. Bellum (war, neuter) is bellum, bellum, bella, bella. Now mix the declensions in sentences. ''Servus dominum laudat'' means ''The servant praises the master'' - servus nominative, dominum accusative. ''Domini servos vocant'' means ''The masters call the servants'' - domini nominative plural, servos accusative plural, vocant ''they call''. ''Puella donum portat'' means ''The girl carries a gift'', where donum is a neuter accusative singular, the same form as its nominative. ''Viri bella non amant'' means ''The men do not love wars'' - viri nominative plural, bella accusative plural neuter, non ''not''."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer or translate, then check. 1) What is the accusative singular of ''amicus'' (friend)? 2) Translate ''Servi dominos vocant.'' 3) Why is ''donum'' the same form whether it is the subject or the object? 4) What is the nominative plural of ''bellum''? 5) Say ''The friend praises the poet'' using amicus, poeta, and laudat. Answers: 1) amicum. 2) ''The servants call the masters'' - servi nominative plural, dominos accusative plural. 3) because it is neuter, and neuter nouns have identical nominative and accusative forms. 4) bella. 5) Amicus poetam laudat - note that poeta, though it looks first declension, is masculine, and here it takes the accusative ending -am to become poetam."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: giving a neuter noun an accusative that differs from its nominative - for neuters the two are always the same (bellum and bellum), and both plurals end in -a (bella). Mistake: forgetting that -i is the masculine nominative plural, so ''servi'' means servants acting as the subject. Mistake: confusing the masculine accusative plural -os (servos) with the nominative plural -i (servi). Exit ticket: (a) What is the accusative singular of ''servus''? (b) What is the nominative plural of ''bellum''? (c) Which ending marks the masculine nominative plural? Answers: servum; bella; the ending -i, as in servi and domini."}]}'::jsonb, 'Second-declension masculines end in -us (accusative -um); neuters end in -um and have the same form in nominative and accusative.', 13),
  (6, 'Present-tense verbs and the verb ''sum''', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to conjugate any first-conjugation verb in the present tense, conjugate the irregular verb sum (to be), and read a sentence in which the verb ending alone tells you who the subject is. Warm-up: if ''amo'' means ''I love'', what might ''amant'' mean? The ending holds the clue. This is one of the most empowering steps in beginning Latin, because a single set of six endings unlocks a very large family of verbs. Once the endings are automatic, you stop translating word by word and start hearing the person and number directly, the way you hear tense in an English verb without stopping to think about it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"First-conjugation verbs have infinitives ending in -are, such as amare (to love), and a present stem in -a (ama-). To that stem you add the personal endings: -o (I), -s (you singular), -t (he, she, or it), -mus (we), -tis (you plural), and -nt (they). So amo, amas, amat, amamus, amatis, amant mean I love, you love, he or she loves, we love, you all love, they love. The same six endings drive porto (I carry), laudo (I praise), voco (I call), specto (I watch), and do (I give). The verb ''to be'', sum, is irregular and simply has to be memorized: sum, es, est, sumus, estis, sunt mean I am, you are, he or she or it is, we are, you all are, they are. Because the ending already names the subject, Latin seldom needs a separate word for ''I'' or ''we''."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"The full present of amare, as a table in words: amo (I love), amas (you love), amat (he or she loves), amamus (we love), amatis (you all love), amant (they love). The full present of sum: sum (I am), es (you are), est (he, she, or it is), sumus (we are), estis (you all are), sunt (they are). Now sentences: ''Puellam amo'' means ''I love the girl'' - no separate word for ''I'' is needed, because -o supplies it. ''Aquam portant'' means ''They carry water''. ''Servus bonus est'' means ''The servant is good'', where est links servus to the adjective bonus. ''Nautae sumus'' means ''We are sailors''. ''Dominum laudatis'' means ''You all praise the master''."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Translate or supply the form, then check. 1) Translate ''amamus.'' 2) Say ''They watch the girls'' using spectare and puellas. 3) What is ''you (singular) are'', from sum? 4) Translate ''Rosas portatis.'' 5) Say ''We are friends'' using amici. 6) What does the ending -t tell you about the subject? Answers: 1) ''we love''. 2) Puellas spectant. 3) es. 4) ''You all carry the roses''. 5) Amici sumus (or Sumus amici). 6) that the subject is third person singular - he, she, or it. Notice again how the ending, not a pronoun, carries the person and number."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: adding a pronoun that the ending already supplies, or mismatching the ending to the subject - a plural subject needs -nt, as in ''puellae amant'' (the girls love). Mistake: treating sum as if it were regular; its forms (sum, es, est, sumus, estis, sunt) are irregular and must be learned by heart. Mistake: confusing the stems, for instance writing ''amamus'' when you mean the singular ''amat''. Exit ticket: (a) What does the ending -mus mean? (b) Give the third-person singular of sum. (c) What is the first-person singular of amare? Answers: it means ''we''; est; amo."}]}'::jsonb, 'A Latin verb''s personal ending (-o, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt) already names the subject, so a separate pronoun is usually unnecessary.', 13),
  (7, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The accusative singular of ''puella'' (girl) is...","options":["puella","puellam","puellae","puellas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"First-declension accusative singular takes -am, giving puellam."},{"prompt":"Which sentence means ''The servant loves the girl''?","options":["Servus puellam amat","Servum puella amat","Servus puella amat","Puellam servus amant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Servus is the nominative subject, puellam the accusative object, and amat the singular verb. ''Servum puella amat'' would reverse the roles."},{"prompt":"Which form of ''amo'' means ''they love''?","options":["amat","amant","amamus","amas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The third-person plural ending -nt gives amant, ''they love''."},{"prompt":"In the verb ''to be'' (sum), the form for ''you (singular) are'' is...","options":["sum","es","est","sumus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The present of sum runs sum, es, est, sumus, estis, sunt; ''you (singular) are'' is es."},{"prompt":"''Bellum'' (war) is a second-declension neuter noun. Its accusative singular is...","options":["bellum","bellus","bello","bella"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Neuter nouns have the same nominative and accusative, so the accusative singular stays bellum."},{"prompt":"In ''Servus puellam amat,'' which word is the subject?","options":["servus","puellam","amat","none of them"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Servus carries the nominative ending -us, so it is the subject; puellam is the accusative object."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (8, 'Adjective agreement and the other cases', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to make an adjective agree with its noun in gender, number, and case, and to recognize the three remaining cases - genitive (of), dative (to or for), and ablative (by, with, or in) - in their simplest uses. Warm-up: if ''servus'' is masculine, which form of ''good'' (bonus, bona, or bonum) should describe it? Agreement is the glue that holds a Latin phrase together, and the three extra cases are what let Latin express possession, giving, and accompaniment without a crowd of little prepositions. We will treat the new cases as an overview here; the goal is to spot them confidently, not yet to drill every ending."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"First-and-second-declension adjectives come in three sets of endings - masculine like -us, feminine like -a, and neuter like -um, as in bonus, bona, bonum (good). An adjective must agree with its noun in gender, number, and case, although the endings need not look identical. Beyond the nominative and accusative, three further cases round out the basics. The genitive shows possession and means ''of'': puellae means ''of the girl'' or ''the girl''s''. The dative marks the indirect object and means ''to'' or ''for'': puellae means ''to or for the girl'', and servo means ''to or for the servant''. The ablative, very often paired with a preposition, covers ''by, with, from, and in'': cum amico means ''with a friend'', and in silva means ''in the forest''. Learn to recognize these cases first, then drill them."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Agreement in the nominative: ''servus bonus'' (a good servant, masculine), ''puella bona'' (a good girl, feminine), and ''bellum magnum'' (a great war, neuter). In the accusative the adjective shifts with the noun: ''puellam bonam'', ''servum bonum''. Note ''poeta bonus'' - poeta is masculine even though it looks first declension, so it takes the masculine ''bonus''. The other cases: genitive, ''liber poetae'' means ''the poet''s book'' or ''the book of the poet''. Dative, ''Servo donum do'' means ''I give a gift to the servant'' - servo dative, donum accusative, do ''I give''. Ablative, ''Cum amicis ambulamus'' means ''We walk with friends'', with amicis in the ablative plural after cum; and ''In terra laborat'' means ''He works on the land''."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Supply the form or translate, then check. 1) Make ''good'' agree with ''puella'' in the nominative. 2) Make ''good'' agree with ''bellum'' in the nominative. 3) Say ''the poet''s rose'' using rosa and the genitive of poeta. 4) Say ''I give water to the sailor'' using the dative of nauta, plus aqua and do. 5) Which case normally follows the preposition ''cum''? Answers: 1) puella bona. 2) bellum bonum. 3) rosa poetae. 4) Nautae aquam do - nautae is the dative, aquam the accusative object. 5) the ablative, as in cum amico and cum amicis. If you match the adjective by gender, number, and case rather than by spelling, these come out right every time."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: matching an adjective to the noun''s spelling instead of its gender, number, and case - ''poeta bona'' is wrong because poeta is masculine, so you need ''poeta bonus''. Mistake: confusing the look-alike form ''puellae'', which can be genitive singular, dative singular, or nominative plural - context decides which. Mistake: forgetting that the ablative usually travels with a preposition such as cum or in. Exit ticket: (a) An adjective must agree with its noun in which three things? (b) Which case means ''of''? (c) Which case is used after ''cum''? Answers: gender, number, and case; the genitive; the ablative."}]}'::jsonb, 'An adjective must match its noun in gender, number, and case, but the two words need not share the same spelling or ending.', 14),
  (9, 'Core vocabulary II: verbs and everyday words', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"amo","translation":"I love (infinitive amare, to love)","example":"Puellam amo. (I love the girl.)"},{"term":"porto","translation":"I carry (infinitive portare)","example":"Aquam porto. (I carry water.)"},{"term":"laudo","translation":"I praise (infinitive laudare)","example":"Dominum laudant. (They praise the master.)"},{"term":"voco","translation":"I call (infinitive vocare)","example":"Amicum voco. (I call my friend.)"},{"term":"specto","translation":"I watch, I look at (infinitive spectare)","example":"Puellas spectat. (He watches the girls.)"},{"term":"do","translation":"I give (infinitive dare)","example":"Servo donum do. (I give a gift to the servant.)"},{"term":"sum","translation":"I am (infinitive esse, to be)","example":"Nauta sum. (I am a sailor.)"},{"term":"et","translation":"and","example":"Puella et servus ambulant. (The girl and the servant walk.)"},{"term":"in","translation":"in or on (with the ablative); into (with the accusative)","example":"In silva sumus. (We are in the forest.)"},{"term":"non","translation":"not","example":"Servus non laborat. (The servant does not work.)"}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (10, 'Building sentences and the legacy of Latin', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to build and translate simple Latin sentences by reading the endings in whatever order they appear, and to explain how Latin became the Romance languages and seeded so much of English vocabulary. Warm-up: in ''Servum puella amat'', find the verb first - now, who is doing the loving? This lesson brings together everything so far - cases, verbs, agreement - into a single dependable method, and then steps back to see why learning Latin pays dividends far beyond Latin itself. The roots you meet here will start jumping out at you in English and in every Romance language you encounter from now on."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A reliable method for translating Latin has four steps. First, find the verb and note its person and number. Second, find the nominative noun - that is your subject. Third, find the accusative noun - that is your direct object. Fourth, fit in any genitive, dative, or ablative extras. Because word order is free, this ending-first habit keeps you accurate where a left-to-right reading would mislead you. As for the bigger picture: Latin never truly died. Its everyday spoken form, often called Vulgar Latin, evolved over centuries into the Romance languages - Italian, Spanish, French, Portuguese, Romanian, and others. English is a Germanic language at its core, but it absorbed an enormous Latin layer through the Church, through scholarship, and above all through Norman French, so well over half of English vocabulary traces back to Latin roots."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Translate step by step. ''Servum puella amat'': the verb is amat (third singular, ''loves''); the nominative is puella (''the girl''); the accusative is servum (''the servant''); so it means ''The girl loves the servant'', even though servum comes first. ''Poeta reginae fabulam narrat'': narrat (''tells''); poeta (nominative, ''the poet''); fabulam (accusative, ''a story''); reginae (dative, ''to the queen''); so it means ''The poet tells a story to the queen''. Now some roots. Latin aqua (water) gives Spanish agua, Italian acqua, French eau, and English aquarium and aquatic. Latin amare (to love) gives Italian amare, Spanish amar, French aimer, and English amorous and amateur. Latin portare (to carry) gives English portable, transport, and export."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Translate or answer, then check. 1) Translate ''Dominum servi laudant.'' 2) Translate ''Agricola filiae donum dat'' (filia means daughter, dative filiae; dat means gives). 3) Name three Romance languages. 4) What Latin word lies behind the English word ''aquarium''? 5) True or false: English is a Romance language. Answers: 1) ''The servants praise the master'' - servi nominative plural, dominum accusative, laudant ''they praise''. 2) ''The farmer gives a gift to his daughter'' - agricola nominative, filiae dative, donum accusative. 3) for example Spanish, Italian, and French (Portuguese and Romanian also count). 4) aqua, meaning water. 5) false - English is Germanic, though it is heavily influenced by Latin."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: translating strictly left to right and assuming the first word must be the subject - always locate the verb and the nominative first, then let the other endings fall into place. Mistake: calling English a Romance language; it is Germanic in structure with a deep Latin vocabulary layer laid over it. Mistake: forgetting the dative when a sentence has someone receiving something, as in filiae donum dat. Exit ticket: (a) What is the first thing to find when translating a Latin sentence? (b) Latin evolved into which family of languages? (c) Is English itself a Romance language? Answers: the verb; the Romance languages; no, English is Germanic but rich in Latin-derived words."}]}'::jsonb, 'To translate Latin, find the verb first, then the nominative subject, then the accusative object - the endings, not the order, tell you who does what.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000008' and l.position = v.pos
);
